{"qid":"Q216786","names":["Chámpa"],"title":"Chámpa","endonym":{"native":"Chăm Pa, Chiêm Thành","lang":"vi"},"capsule":"Champa (Chăm Pa) was a series of Austronesian-speaking Cham polities along the central and southern coast of present-day Vietnam, tracing its origin to the kingdom of Lâm Ấp (Linyi), founded c. 192 CE in a revolt against Han commandery rule, with its great Hindu sanctuary at Mỹ Sơn. From the 9th-10th century Champa contended with the Khmer Empire to its west and Đại Việt to its north, even sacking Angkor in 1177, but sustained Vietnamese southward expansion steadily ground it down. Lê Thánh Tông's campaign of 1471 stormed the capital Vijaya and annihilated northern Champa, leaving only the southern principalities of Kauthara and Panduranga; the Nguyễn lords then annexed Kauthara in 1653, reducing Cham sovereignty to the rump Panduranga that survived until 1832.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The northern Cham capitals (Indrapura, Vijaya) are archaeologically documented; the full extent of Cham territorial control at any given date is poorly attested and the dataset geometry likely represents scholarly reconstruction from brick-tower distributions and Chinese diplomatic records rather than administrative boundaries.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":"place/Champa-ancient-kingdom-Indochina","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Champa","rulers":[{"qid":"Q5433550","name":"Bhadravarman I","start":380,"end":413,"image":null,"nativeName":"Phạm Hồ Đạt","nativeLang":"vi"},{"qid":"Q11617135","name":"Gangaraja","start":413,"end":415,"image":null,"nativeName":"Phạm Địch Chớn","nativeLang":"vi"},{"